Cygwin documentation is available on the net at https://cygwin.com
You might especially be interested in

https://cygwin.com/faq/faq.html#faq.programming.building-cygwin

ARM64 MSYS2 cross build
======================

build-arm64.sh provides separate prepare, headers, newlib, runtime, install,
utilities and cygpath phases.  This separation allows GCC's libgcc and
freestanding C++ headers to be bootstrapped between the runtime's header and
library phases.  The target is
aarch64-pc-cygwin, not aarch64-w64-mingw32.  The driver must use the Windows
ARM64 LP64 ABI with 64-bit long double and pointer-sized va_list in both C and
C++; the script checks the actual compilers before publishing headers or
building target code.  Compiler ABI changes require rebuilding newlib, the
runtime, CRT objects and applications together, not merely relinking old objects.
The fenv trap-control exports use newlib's real FPCR operations.  Trap-enable
bits may be read-as-zero/write-ignored by the target hardware or OS; exporting
those functions is not a guarantee that hardware floating-point traps work.

Required host tools include Bash, GNU make, GNU Awk, Perl, Automake, Autoconf
2.69 (for newlib), and Autoconf for winsup.  The toolchain prefix must provide
the target C/C++ compiler, binutils, compatible w32api headers/import libraries,
and configured freestanding C++ headers.  libgcc must be installed before the
runtime link.  The build script does not install host dependencies.

For a Windows worktree, run export-arm64-source.ps1 with a new OutputDirectory.
It exports the exact HEAD, tracked binary patch, every untracked source file,
the complete source-file inventory, version metadata and SHA-256 checksums.
import-arm64-source.sh takes that export directory and a NEW WSL source
directory, checks the export, applies the changes and compares the full file
inventory before hashing the imported source.  It never reads the Windows
worktree's .git pointer with WSL git.  Keep newly authored shell sources LF-only.

In WSL, load the adjacent SOURCE.env produced by the importer, then invoke:

  bash SOURCE/winsup/build-arm64.sh prepare SOURCE BUILD_ROOT TOOLCHAIN_PREFIX
  bash SOURCE/winsup/build-arm64.sh headers SOURCE BUILD_ROOT TOOLCHAIN_PREFIX

After the compiler's libgcc and C++ header bootstrap:

  bash SOURCE/winsup/build-arm64.sh newlib SOURCE BUILD_ROOT TOOLCHAIN_PREFIX
  bash SOURCE/winsup/build-arm64.sh runtime SOURCE BUILD_ROOT TOOLCHAIN_PREFIX
  bash SOURCE/winsup/build-arm64.sh install SOURCE BUILD_ROOT TOOLCHAIN_PREFIX

JOBS defaults to 2.  SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH, MSYS2_RUNTIME_COMMIT and
UNAME_DEV_VERSION are required and are supplied by the exported metadata.
SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH also enables the existing deterministic archive and
--no-insert-timestamp DLL rules.  Do not disable DYNAMIC_BASE on Windows ARM64.
CFLAGS and CXXFLAGS default to -g -O2 and can be supplied explicitly.  Debug
paths are build inputs; use matching absolute paths or GCC debug-prefix maps
when comparing complete rebuilds in different build directories.

reproduce-arm64.sh SOURCE TOOLCHAIN_PREFIX NEW_BUILD_ROOT performs two clean
builds using canonical debug/macro paths and fixed build-user/hostname metadata.
It compares libc, libm, generated TLS/assembly/object files, libdll.a, the DLL
and its import library byte-for-byte, and writes SHA256SUMS only for matching
pairs.  It does not install a runtime DLL or claim native execution coverage.
Pass a fourth argument naming an already qualified newlib build directory
to compare runtime-only rebuilds without rebuilding newlib.  Its archives and
generated header are hashed before copying and checked in both build roots.
Always use a private toolchain prefix; the full-build mode installs headers.

The runtime personality is explicit in configure's generated config.h and
the common preprocessor flags; it must not depend on compiler predefines.
Startup objects also receive the MSYS flags.  The resource rule forwards
source/build preprocessor include paths to windres.  Runtime links honor
LDFLAGS, including additional library search paths, and record each actual
invocation under new-msys-2.0.dll.link.*/ with a replayable command.sh,
NUL-separated argv, stdout/stderr, exit status and successful output hash.
Keep these records with the source, compiler and complete link-input hashes.
Do not infer a historical command from a silent "CXXLD" build-log line.

Before rebuilding a recovered tree, copy any known-good tlsoffsets and verify
its digest.  Reconfigure and build only in a new private copy: copied Makefiles
can still contain absolute paths into their source or previous build.
gentls_offsets publishes atomically only after every compiler-emitted member
has a valid .long/.word value; a failed generation preserves the old file.

The normal Automake targets build libdll.a and link new-msys-2.0.dll with
dll_entry and msys.def.  Installation provides bin/msys-2.0.dll and
aarch64-pc-cygwin/lib/libmsys-2.0.a, CRT objects and headers.  Never hand-select
successful objects or substitute historical TLS offsets for a failed build.
The generated offsets must come from the current compiler and headers.

Application builds require an MSYS-configured driver/specs layer: an unmodified
Cygwin driver requests libcygwin instead of libmsys-2.0.  Do not disguise that
configuration error with a renamed import library.  Confirm a normally linked
native application against the installed DLL before building dependent tools.
The utilities phase builds and installs cygserver and the POSIX utilities with
the prefix's msys2-gcc/msys2-g++ drivers after runtime installation; it may also
require hosted C++ headers/libraries and additional w32api imports.  The runtime
phase builds the complete DLL, CRT and import libraries, not those applications.

To build only cygpath against an already qualified runtime/SDK, use a new
private copy of that prefix and a prepared source snapshot from the same
runtime cohort:

  bash SOURCE/winsup/build-arm64.sh cygpath SOURCE NEW_BUILD_ROOT PRIVATE_PREFIX

This configures winsup, builds the maintained utils/cygpath.exe target with
msys2-g++ and installs bin/cygpath.exe without a cross-tool name prefix.
It does not rebuild or install the runtime, newlib, CRT or C headers.  The
utility link is timestamp-free and requires libnetapi32.a, libuserenv.a and
libntdll.a in addition to the paired MSYS runtime/CRT and C++ SDK inputs.
These must be genuine ARM64 Windows API import libraries, not a substitute
MinGW CRT or sysroot.  Run native path conversions and verify the loaded
runtime; a successful cross build alone does not qualify the utility.

Host generator regression:

  bash winsup/testsuite/build/gentls_offsets.sh

Additional host-only build and startup failure-path checks:

  bash winsup/testsuite/build/runtime_build.sh
  bash winsup/testsuite/build/startup_safety.sh

The latter compiles the production allocation and copy routines against
deterministic Win32 call stubs using CXX_FOR_BUILD (g++ by default).
The native cygheap-guard test separately checks the reserved no-access gap
between the stack arena and fixed cygheap, committed heap memory, and thread
stack bounds.  The gap is allocation-granularity sized and remains reserved;
unlike PAGE_GUARD it cannot be cleared by the first fault.

Native tests include winsup.api/setjmp (return values and nonvolatile state)
and winsup.api/signal-context-arm64 (asynchronous vector-register restoration).
Host fixture success alone is not a runtime, fork/exec or application result.
